1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,460
Open your camera's menu

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:03,903
and you'll find a number:

3
00:00:03,703 --> 00:00:06,442
twenty-three point nine eight.

4
00:00:06,692 --> 00:00:10,994
Hidden inside it are two decisions made a century ago.

5
00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:14,681
August, nineteen twenty-six.

6
00:00:14,481 --> 00:00:15,438
New York.

7
00:00:15,688 --> 00:00:17,546
At the Warners' Theatre

8
00:00:17,346 --> 00:00:19,205
the film Don Juan opens.

9
00:00:20,814 --> 00:00:22,581
As the screen lights up

10
00:00:22,381 --> 00:00:24,615
an orchestra begins to play.

11
00:00:24,415 --> 00:00:27,372
But there isn't a single musician in the pit.

12
00:00:27,873 --> 00:00:30,989
The music is coming from the projection booth

13
00:00:30,789 --> 00:00:34,213
from a giant record spinning beside the projector

14
00:00:34,013 --> 00:00:35,825
driven by the same motor.

15
00:00:37,074 --> 00:00:39,797
Warner Bros., called it Vitaphone.

16
00:00:40,972 --> 00:00:42,698
But it had a weakness.

17
00:00:42,498 --> 00:00:44,223
If the needle skipped

18
00:00:44,023 --> 00:00:46,158
the sound ran ahead of the picture

19
00:00:45,958 --> 00:00:47,698
or fell behind.

20
00:00:49,390 --> 00:00:51,418
Then the projectionist had to put

21
00:00:51,218 --> 00:00:53,182
the needle back in place by hand.

22
00:00:54,316 --> 00:00:56,220
And there was another problem.

23
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:59,756
Every theater ran its projectors at a different speed.

24
00:01:02,103 --> 00:01:05,474
The Vitaphone equipment was built by Western Electric.

25
00:01:06,341 --> 00:01:07,889
One of its engineers

26
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:09,000
Stanley Watkins

27
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,062
asked a projectionist how fast

28
00:01:10,862 --> 00:01:13,362
theaters actually ran their film.

29
00:01:15,749 --> 00:01:19,266
The answer:, big first-run houses

30
00:01:19,066 --> 00:01:21,470
eighty to ninety feet a minute.

31
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:24,251
Small theaters, over a hundred.

32
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:27,066
Watkins picked one number in between.

33
00:01:26,866 --> 00:01:29,171
Ninety feet a minute.

34
00:01:30,359 --> 00:01:33,173
Years later, he recalled it in an interview.

35
00:01:32,973 --> 00:01:35,391
"That had to be a good guess."

36
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:39,325
One foot of thirty-five millimeter

37
00:01:39,125 --> 00:01:41,035
film holds sixteen frames.

38
00:01:40,835 --> 00:01:44,650
Ninety feet a minute is twenty-four frames a second.

39
00:01:48,079 --> 00:01:49,545
The next October

40
00:01:49,345 --> 00:01:52,688
The Jazz Singer let audiences hear an actor sing and speak

41
00:01:52,488 --> 00:01:55,787
and the talkies arrived.

42
00:01:56,037 --> 00:01:59,837
Fox Movietone, which printed sound onto the film itself

43
00:01:59,637 --> 00:02:01,675
chose the same twenty-four.

44
00:02:01,925 --> 00:02:05,060
Twenty-four became the standard of the cinema.

45
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:07,329
The trouble would start outside it.

46
00:02:08,903 --> 00:02:11,009
Nineteen fifty-three.

47
00:02:10,809 --> 00:02:14,902
Many American homes already had a black-and-white TV.

48
00:02:15,152 --> 00:02:19,590
Black-and-white broadcasts sent thirty pictures a second.

49
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:23,588
What broadcasters prepared next was color.

50
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:25,391
In nineteen fifty

51
00:02:25,191 --> 00:02:28,887
the FCC approved CBS's color system.

52
00:02:28,687 --> 00:02:31,822
But the sets people already owned couldn't show it.

53
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:38,099
In March nineteen fifty-three

54
00:02:37,899 --> 00:02:39,830
CBS gave it up.

55
00:02:41,669 --> 00:02:44,017
On December seventeenth that year

56
00:02:43,817 --> 00:02:48,565
the FCC approved a new color system

57
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:50,342
that old sets could still show:

58
00:02:50,142 --> 00:02:50,754
NTSC.

59
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:53,967
The trick was to tuck the color inside

60
00:02:53,767 --> 00:02:56,390
the existing black-and-white signal.

61
00:02:57,292 --> 00:02:58,845
But there was a catch.

62
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:02,704
The color signal sat too close to the sound signal.

63
00:03:04,487 --> 00:03:06,882
The clash showed up as a fine pattern

64
00:03:06,682 --> 00:03:09,149
of dots on black-and-white screens.

65
00:03:09,535 --> 00:03:11,861
Moving the sound would fix it

66
00:03:11,661 --> 00:03:15,182
but every set already sold would lose its sound.

67
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:16,961
The FCC wouldn't allow it.

68
00:03:20,826 --> 00:03:24,122
The NTSC committee's engineers went the other way.

69
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:26,163
They slowed the picture down

70
00:03:25,963 --> 00:03:27,217
just slightly.

71
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:32,041
Old sets followed the timing of the broadcast signal

72
00:03:31,841 --> 00:03:35,440
so a slowdown of a tenth of a percent didn't bother them.

73
00:03:36,566 --> 00:03:40,961
The picture slowed by a thousand over a thousand and one.

74
00:03:40,761 --> 00:03:45,105
Thirty frames a second became twenty-nine point nine seven.

75
00:03:46,005 --> 00:03:47,901
So what happened to the movies?

76
00:03:49,146 --> 00:03:51,900
To broadcast twenty-four frame films

77
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,553
on twenty-nine point nine seven TV

78
00:03:54,353 --> 00:03:57,169
the films had to slow down by the same amount.

79
00:03:59,157 --> 00:04:03,461
Twenty-four, times a thousand over a thousand and one:

80
00:04:03,261 --> 00:04:06,723
twenty-three point nine seven six.

81
00:04:07,623 --> 00:04:10,011
And now, back to your camera's menu.

82
00:04:11,793 --> 00:04:16,246
Twenty-three point nine eight is two decisions multiplied:

83
00:04:16,046 --> 00:04:19,146
the speed Watkins chose in a projection booth

84
00:04:18,946 --> 00:04:22,763
and the slowdown that protected the TVs already sold.

85
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:32,125
Next time:, Kodak built a digital camera in nineteen seventy-five

86
00:04:31,925 --> 00:04:33,940
and never sold it.

87
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:36,946
We'll look at what the records say about why.
